<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>Give a point <m>P</m> with whole-number coordinates that is the same distance from the line <m>x = {1}</m> as from the line <m>y = {-2}</m>, and is not on either line.</p><div><m>P = \big(</m><fillin characters="5" name="ans1"/>, <fillin characters="5" name="ans2"/><m>\big)</m>   </div></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>The lines <m>x = {1}</m> and <m>y = {-2}</m> cross at right angles at <m>({1}, {-2})</m>. The points equidistant from them lie on the two bisectors of the angles between them: the lines through <m>({1}, {-2})</m> with gradients <m>1</m> and <m>-1</m>. Start at <m>({1}, {-2})</m> and move the same number of units across and up (or down), for example <m>({3}, {0})</m>, which is <m>2</m> units from each line, or <m>({-2}, {1})</m>, which is <m>3</m> units from each.</p></p></solution>
    <answer><p><m>3</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>0</m></p></answer>
</stack-static>